ee versus $8,000 for Russell", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win near 96 percent"], "rationale": "Every available signal points the same direction: the district's Democratic lean, Pingree's incumbency and fundraising dominance, a 22-point September poll from a reputable pollster, and markets near 96 percent. With only one public poll and no expert ratings, uncertainty stays moderate, but a Republican win would require a historic shift.", "watch": "A major late-breaking event or a new poll showing a far tighter race would be the main signal to revisit, though nothing in the current evidence suggests movement toward Russell.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Incumbent Chellie Pingree (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Chellie Pingree seeks another term in a Maine district that leans Democratic by about eleven points at the presidential level. She has held the seat since 2009 and enters the fall with a large financial edge: roughly $320,000 on hand compared with about $8,000 for Ronald Russell, the Republican nominee in a rematch. A September poll of likely voters from the University of New Hampshire put her ahead 57 to 35, and prediction markets price her chances near 96 percent.\n\nCampaign coverage has centered on the rematch rather than on any scandal or external event, and no outside spending targets the incumbent. With fundamentals, polling, money, and markets all aligned, the race sits firmly in the safe Democratic column. The main uncertainty is the thin public polling record, with a single general-election survey available, so a late shift cannot be fully ruled out.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.989, "margin": 20.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9938, "margin": 20.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9879, "margin": 20.9}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9946, "margin": 21.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9957, "margin": 21.5}, {"date": "